<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Four Macedonian Republics</h1><p class="st-meta">167 BCE – 146 BCE · capital Amphipolis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The four self-governing districts into which Rome split the old kingdom of Macedon after Pydna, a short-lived experiment that ended in revolt and annexation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Pydna</a> in 168 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> abolished the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia</a>. In 167 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Lucius Aemilius Paullus</a> announced the settlement at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/amphipolis">Amphipolis</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> was to be 'free' but divided into four republics (merides) with capitals at Amphipolis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pella">Pella</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each republic had elected magistrates and a council, but contact between them was restricted: citizens could not marry or own land across the borders. The Macedonians paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> half the taxes they had paid their kings, the royal gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines were closed until 158 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> from the royal forests was banned. The first meris struck silver coins showing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Resentment and disorder grew. In 149 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriscus">Andriscus</a>, claiming to be a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a>, reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> briefly as king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-macedonicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus</a> defeated him in 148 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Macedonia</a> became a Roman province in 146 BCE, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a> later binding it together.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 167 BCE – 146 BCE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:56.38%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>167 BCE – 146 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amphipolis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Koine Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>, <a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Macedonian merides, Macedonian republics</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-156&amp;focus=four-macedonian-republics" aria-label="Four Macedonian Republics on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5264 720 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Four Macedonian Republics lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5584 976L5552 978M5584 976L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5552 978h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5584 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5584 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Amphipolis. <a href="/atlas